  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/sundadanio-retiarius-2/",
      "html": "<p>The dwarf fish of the genus Sundadanio only grow to 1.5–2 cm long. They are strongly adapted to blackwater and require careful acclimatisation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/sundadanio-retiarius-2/",
      "html": "<p>Sundadanio retiarius comes from Borneo (Central Kalimantan).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/sundadanio-retiarius-2/",
      "html": "<p>The parasite Piscinoodinium especially makes life difficult for the tiny fish and is also very difficult to detect on their small bodies. Once successfully acclimated, Sundadanio are surprisingly tough fish that you can enjoy for many years.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/sundadanio-retiarius-2/",
      "html": "<p>The males can be recognized by the black front anal fin rays; they also grow larger than the females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/sundadanio-retiarius-2/",
      "html": "<p>For many years only one species was known, which found its way into the aquarium as Rasbora axelrodi, and in which different colour varieties were distinguished. There is now a consensus that these are not variants but independent species. Sundadanio retiarius was known in the hobby as Rasbora axelrodi \"Red\" before its scientific naming.</p>"
    }
  ],
